        {
          "unit_id": "pitch_prep",
          "title": "The Night Before",
          "description": "Convierte un inglés coloquial en formal: nominalización y registro de negocios. Y marcadores para estructurar un argumento: nevertheless, that said, mind you, on top of that.",
          "lessons": [
            {
              "lesson_id": "pitch_prep_lesson",
              "title": "Nominalisation and formal register",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "phase": "context",
                  "type": "audio_context",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "The night before",
                    "dialogue": [
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Slide four says: \"We grew really fast last year.\" Too casual?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Much too casual. Let's say: \"The studio experienced rapid growth last year.\""
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Fine. Slide five: \"Clients booked more rooms because the sites were easier to use.\""
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "\"Improved usability led to a significant increase in bookings.\""
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "You sound like Helen. Slide six: \"We want to open an office in Madrid.\""
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "\"Our intention is to expand into the Spanish market.\" Mark loves the word \"expand\"."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "And this one: \"We decided to work with Grace because she understood us.\""
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "\"Our decision to partner with Grace was based on a shared vision.\""
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Do people really talk like this?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "In boardrooms they do. Tomorrow we speak their language. Tonight, we speak ours."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "In that case, my proposal is the immediate acquisition of a pizza."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Proposal accepted."
                      }
                    ],
                    "passage": "May. New York, the night before the pitch. Lucía and Daniel are in their hotel room, rewriting the presentation for Mark Reed's board."
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "discovery",
                  "type": "multiple_choice",
                  "content": {
                    "instructions": "Fíjate en el diálogo y responde.",
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "question": "\"The studio experienced rapid growth\" vs \"We grew really fast\". The formal version uses…",
                        "options": [
                          "a noun (growth)",
                          "a verb (grew)"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"…led to a significant increase in bookings.\" Here increase is…",
                        "options": [
                          "a noun",
                          "a verb"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"Our intention is to expand…\" = …",
                        "options": [
                          "We intend to expand…",
                          "We are expanding now…"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "What does Daniel propose at the end?",
                        "options": [
                          "ordering a pizza",
                          "cancelling the pitch",
                          "going to bed"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Nominalisation and formal register",
                    "explanation": "En informes, presentaciones y textos académicos el inglés formal convierte verbos y adjetivos en nombres. A eso se le llama nominalización, y hace el texto más objetivo y denso:\ngrow → growth · decide → decision · intend → intention · improve → improvement · fail → failure · increase → an increase.\n\nPatrones típicos: the + nombre + of (the failure of the old site), a + adjetivo + nombre (a significant increase), lead to / result in (provocar).\n\nCompara: We grew really fast (coloquial) → The studio experienced rapid growth (formal).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Coloquial",
                        "Formal (nombre)",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "grow fast",
                          "rapid growth",
                          "The studio experienced rapid growth."
                        ],
                        [
                          "decide",
                          "decision",
                          "Our decision to partner with Grace…"
                        ],
                        [
                          "intend",
                          "intention",
                          "Our intention is to expand."
                        ],
                        [
                          "increase",
                          "an increase in",
                          "a significant increase in bookings"
                        ],
                        [
                          "improve",
                          "improvement",
                          "a clear improvement in speed"
                        ],
                        [
                          "easy to use",
                          "usability",
                          "Improved usability led to more bookings."
                        ],
                        [
                          "fail",
                          "failure",
                          "The failure of the old site…"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "The studio experienced rapid growth last year.",
                      "Improved usability led to a significant increase in bookings.",
                      "Our intention is to expand into the Spanish market.",
                      "Our decision to partner with Grace was based on a shared vision."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Signposting: nevertheless, that said, mind you, on top of that",
                    "explanation": "Para guiar al oyente en un argumento largo:\n\nRegistro formal (presentaciones, informes):\n• Nevertheless / Nonetheless (aun así): Our studio is small. Nevertheless, we deliver on time.\n• Admittedly (hay que reconocer que): Admittedly, our prices are higher.\n• To a certain extent / To what extent…: To a certain extent, design drives bookings.\n• In so far as (en la medida en que): The plan worked in so far as it was applied.\n• Whereas (mientras que): Madrid is growing fast, whereas London is saturated.\n\nRegistro informal (conversación, correos entre colegas):\n• That said (dicho esto): It's expensive. That said, it's worth it.\n• Mind you (aunque, eso sí): Mark loves the idea. Mind you, he loves most ideas.\n• On top of that (encima, además): We were late, and on top of that the projector broke.\n• As I was saying / Anyway, back to…: para retomar el hilo.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Función",
                        "Formal",
                        "Informal"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Contraste",
                          "nevertheless, nonetheless",
                          "that said, mind you"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Concesión",
                          "admittedly",
                          "OK, fair enough"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Añadir",
                          "moreover, furthermore",
                          "on top of that, what's more"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Grado",
                          "to a certain extent, in so far as",
                          "kind of, more or less"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Retomar",
                          "returning to…",
                          "anyway, as I was saying"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Admittedly, Green Door is a young company. Nevertheless, our results speak for themselves.",
                      "The Madrid market is growing, whereas London is close to saturation.",
                      "The deadline is tight. That said, we've done it before.",
                      "Mark seemed happy. Mind you, he always seems happy before he negotiates."
                    ]
                  }
                },
